Q: No &#39;Access-Control-Allow-Origin&#39; header is present on the requested resource. While deploying backend api A: <p>You'll need to add <code>https://auth-backend-livid.vercel.app</code> as an allowed origin:</p>
<pre><code>app.use(cors({
    origin: 'https://auth-backend-livid.vercel.app',
    methods: '*',
    allowedHeaders: '*'
}));
</code></pre>
<p>The trick with CORS is to specify at least one origin, otherwise specifing <code>*</code> for allowed origins, allowed methods, and allowed headers essentially means &quot;don't use CORS&quot;. The server doesn't send any CORS headers at all — at least that's how a .NET backend behaves when enabling CORS, so I'm wondering if that's how your Node backend is behaving as well.</p>

Q: Flutter isolate &quot;Invalid argument(s): Illegal argument in isolate message: (object extends NativeWrapper - Library:&#39;dart:ui&#39; Class: EngineLayer)&quot; A: <p>Your case:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>import &quot;dart:async&quot;;
import &quot;dart:isolate&quot;;

void main() async {
  final unsendable = Future.value(42);
  final args = ['Hello', 'Goodbye'];
  Isolate.run(() async {
    foo(args);
  });

  Timer(Duration(seconds: 1), () {
    // Capture an unsendable `Future` object
    print(unsendable.hashCode);
  });
}

void foo(List arg) {
  print(arg);
}

</code></pre>
<p>Result:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Invalid argument(s): Illegal argument in isolate message: object is unsendable - Library:'dart:async' Class: _Future@5048458
</code></pre>
<p>Another way:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>import &quot;dart:async&quot;;
import &quot;dart:isolate&quot;;

import &quot;package:multitasking/work/isolated_work.dart&quot;;

void main() async {
  final unsendable = Future.value(42);
  final args = ['Hello', 'Goodbye'];
  unawaited(() async {
    final work = IsolatedWork.withArgument(args, foo);
    final result = await work.run();
    print('List length: $result');
  }());

  // Or exactly as in your code
  // IsolatedWork.withArgument(args, foo).run();

  Timer(Duration(seconds: 1), () {
    // Capture an unsendable `Future` object
    print(unsendable.hashCode);
  });
}

int foo(List arg) {
  print(arg);
  return arg.length;
}

</code></pre>
<p>Result:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>[Hello, Goodbye]
List length: 2
833230290
</code></pre>

Q: status bar icons not showing up as dark A: <p>The most effective solution is to set</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>isAppearanceLightStatusBars = !darkTheme
isAppearanceLightNavigationBars = !darkTheme
</code></pre>
<p>in your</p>
<pre><code>StatusBarTestTheme
</code></pre>
<p>This is how I solved it in <a href="https://github.com/mobiledevpro/closetalk.app" rel="nofollow noreferrer">my app template</a> :</p>
<pre><code>@Composable
fun AppTheme(
    darkTheme: Boolean = true,
    content: @Composable () -&gt; Unit
) {
    val colorScheme = when {
        darkTheme -&gt; DarkColorScheme
        else -&gt; LightColorScheme
    }
    val view = LocalView.current
    if (!view.isInEditMode) {
        SideEffect {
            val window = (view.context as Activity).window
            val windowInsetsController =
                WindowCompat.getInsetsController(window, view)

            windowInsetsController.apply {
                isAppearanceLightStatusBars = !darkTheme
                isAppearanceLightNavigationBars = !darkTheme
            }
        }
    }

    MaterialTheme(
        colorScheme = colorScheme,
        typography = Typography
    ) {
        Box(
            modifier = Modifier
                .fillMaxSize()
                .background(color = MaterialTheme.colorScheme.surfaceContainer)
        ) {
            content()
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And set statusbar transparent in themes.xml</p>
<pre><code>&lt;resources&gt;

    &lt;style name=&quot;Theme.AppTemplateCompose&quot; parent=&quot;android:Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar&quot;&gt;
        &lt;item name=&quot;android:fitsSystemWindows&quot;&gt;false&lt;/item&gt;
        &lt;item name=&quot;android:statusBarColor&quot;&gt;@android:color/transparent&lt;/item&gt;
        &lt;item name=&quot;android:navigationBarColor&quot;&gt;@android:color/transparent&lt;/item&gt;

        &lt;!-- This allows to set the status bar fully transparent. It was removed in androidx.core:core-splashscreen 1.2.0 --&gt;
        &lt;item name=&quot;android:enforceNavigationBarContrast&quot;&gt;false&lt;/item&gt;
        &lt;item name=&quot;android:enforceStatusBarContrast&quot;&gt;false&lt;/item&gt;
    &lt;/style&gt;

    &lt;style name=&quot;Theme.AppTemplateCompose.Splash&quot; parent=&quot;Theme.SplashScreen&quot;&gt;
        &lt;item name=&quot;windowSplashScreenBackground&quot;&gt;@color/launcher_background&lt;/item&gt;
        &lt;item name=&quot;windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon&quot;&gt;@drawable/ic_splash&lt;/item&gt;
        &lt;item name=&quot;postSplashScreenTheme&quot;&gt;@style/Theme.AppTemplateCompose&lt;/item&gt;
    &lt;/style&gt;
&lt;/resources&gt;
</code></pre>
